  "studyGuide": "\n<h3>Module 8 — Reading Food Labels: Quick Reference</h3>\n<p><strong>Core idea:</strong> The front of the package is advertising; the back is information. Read the label top to bottom, and always check the serving size first.</p>\n<p><strong>Order:</strong> Serving size · servings per container · calories · fat (saturated, trans) · cholesterol · sodium · carbohydrate (fiber, total sugars, added sugars) · protein · vitamin D, calcium, iron, potassium.</p>\n<p><strong>Serving size:</strong> Every number is per serving. Multiply by how much you actually ate.</p>\n<p><strong>%DV — 5% / 20% rule:</strong> 5% or less = low. 20% or more = high. Aim high for fiber, vitamin D, calcium, iron, potassium. Aim low for saturated fat, sodium, added sugars.</p>\n<p><strong>Ingredient list:</strong> Listed by weight, most to least. Added sugars hide under many names (high-fructose corn syrup, honey, dextrose, maltose). Whole grain must be the first ingredient (\"whole wheat flour\").</p>\n<p><strong>Claims:</strong> Low-fat = 3 g or less per serving. Low-sodium = 140 mg or less. High-fiber = 5 g or more. \"Natural\" and \"multigrain\" mean little.</p>\n<p><strong>Self-check:</strong> Read a label top to bottom, scale by serving size, apply the 5% / 20% rule, spot added sugars and whole grains, and explain three claims. If you can, you have this module.</p>\n"
